 𝗕𝗮𝘀𝗵 𝗧𝗶𝗽: 𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗽𝘁 -𝘀 𝗻𝘂𝗹𝗹𝗴𝗹𝗼𝗯

Ever written a Bash loop expecting it to iterate over files, but instead it treated your pattern as a literal string?

Example:

𝘧𝘪𝘭𝘦𝘴=(/𝘱𝘢𝘵𝘩/𝘵𝘰/𝘥𝘪𝘳/*.𝘵𝘹𝘵)
𝘦𝘤𝘩𝘰 "𝘍𝘰𝘶𝘯𝘥 ${hashtag#𝘧𝘪𝘭𝘦𝘴[@]} 𝘧𝘪𝘭𝘦𝘴"

👉 If there are .txt files, great — they’re added to the files array.

👉 But if there are no .txt files, Bash doesn’t return an empty array.
Instead, it literally stores the string *.txt as one item in the array.
So files has a length of 1, even though no files exist.

This can lead to false counts, wrong loops, and bugs.

✅ The fix: enable nullglob

𝘴𝘩𝘰𝘱𝘵 -𝘴 𝘯𝘶𝘭𝘭𝘨𝘭𝘰𝘣

With this option set, Bash will expand to nothing if there are no matches.
Now your array is truly empty when there are no .txt files, and your script behaves as expected.
